<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="practice-fundamental-theorem">

  <title>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</title>

  <exercise xml:id="practice-ftc-1">
    <title>Differentiating an Integral</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus and the chain rule to
        determine <m>dy/dx</m> for each function.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>
            y=\int_1^{\sqrt{x}}\ln(t)\,dt.
          </m>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus and the chain rule,
        </p>

        <me>
          \frac{dy}{dx}
          =
          \ln(\sqrt{x})\frac{d}{dx}(\sqrt{x})
          =
          \frac{\ln(\sqrt{x})}{2\sqrt{x}}.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>
            y=\int_{\sin(x)}^0 \tan(u)\,du.
          </m>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Since the variable limit is the lower limit, the Fundamental
          Theorem of Calculus gives
        </p>

        <me>
          \frac{dy}{dx}
          =
          -\tan(\sin x)\frac{d}{dx}(\sin x)
          =
          -\tan(\sin x)\cos x.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>

</section>